# C1.1: 001–016. The applied tasks deliberately distinguish catalysis from energetics.
b.add('C1.1','Catalysis without consumption','A purified enzyme is added to a reaction mixture. The same amount of product is eventually formed with or without the enzyme, but the enzyme-treated mixture reaches this amount sooner. Which conclusion is supported?',refs=[1,10],difficulty='easy',options=['The enzyme provides a pathway with lower activation energy.','The enzyme increases the energy released per reaction.','The enzyme becomes a permanent part of every product molecule.','The enzyme supplies all the energy used to break substrate bonds.'],reason='A catalyst lowers activation energy and increases reaction rate. It does not change the overall energy difference between substrates and products or become consumed stoichiometrically.')
b.add('C1.1','A branched metabolic network','An original simplified metabolic network is shown. Each labelled arrow is catalysed by a different enzyme. A mutation eliminates enzyme E2; E1, E3 and E4 remain functional. Assume no alternative pathway supplies C.',refs=[2,3],diagram=SVG(box(35,145,100,60,'A')+box(250,145,100,60,'B')+box(550,70,100,60,'C')+box(550,245,100,60,'D')+arrow(140,175,245,175)+txt(190,150,'E1')+arrow(355,160,545,105)+txt(435,110,'E2')+arrow(355,190,545,270)+txt(440,257,'E3')+box(250,290,100,60,'E')+arrow(300,210,300,284)+txt(338,253,'E4'), 'Original branched metabolic pathway with separate enzyme steps'),parts=[P('Predict the direct effect on production of C and explain why production of D need not stop.',A('Production of C decreases or stops because the B-to-C step lacks its catalyst.'),R('E1 and E3 can still convert A through B to D; different enzymes catalyse different branches.')),P('Compound D is a polymer assembled from smaller monomers. Classify its synthesis and name a contrasting type of reaction.',A('Its synthesis is anabolic; it builds a larger molecule from smaller units.'),A('Catabolism breaks larger molecules into smaller ones, for example hydrolysis during digestion.'))])
b.add('C1.1','Active-site change at a distance','A replacement of one amino acid far from an enzyme’s active site reduces its activity, although none of the active-site amino acids has been replaced.',refs=[4,5,7],parts=[P('Explain how this substitution can reduce activity.',R('The substitution can alter interactions stabilizing the overall three-dimensional fold.'),R('The changed fold alters the position or chemical properties of active-site residues, reducing binding or catalysis.')),P('Explain why an induced-fit model is more appropriate than a completely rigid active-site model.',R('Binding changes the conformation of the enzyme and substrate.'),R('These changes bring catalytic groups into an effective arrangement or strain substrate bonds, facilitating catalysis.'))])
b.add('C1.1','Immobilized enzyme beads','Lactase is immobilized in porous beads. The beads convert lactose in a stirred solution to products. A student claims that immobilization prevents all enzyme–substrate collisions because the enzyme cannot move freely.',refs=[5,6],difficulty='easy',parts=[P('Explain why the claim is incorrect.',R('Lactose molecules move randomly in solution and can diffuse into pores to reach active sites.'),R('A collision requires relative movement; the substrate can move even if the enzyme is fixed.')),P('Suggest one reason why faster stirring could increase the observed rate without changing the enzyme’s molecular structure.',A('Stirring reduces the concentration boundary layer around beads or replenishes substrate near them, improving substrate supply. Accept removal of accumulated products where justified.'))])
b.add('C1.1','Heating and recovery','Constructed results show the activity of one enzyme after separate samples were held at different temperatures for ten minutes, then all assayed at 25 °C in identical substrate and buffer conditions.'+T(['Pretreatment / °C','Rate at 25 °C / arbitrary units'],[[5,98],[25,100],[45,95],[70,8]]),refs=[7,8],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Explain why the low rate after 70 °C pretreatment suggests a persistent structural change rather than only slower molecular motion.',R('All rates were measured at the same temperature, so current temperature-dependent molecular motion is controlled.'),R('High-temperature treatment can disrupt interactions maintaining enzyme shape, leaving an altered active site after cooling.')),P('State a limitation of concluding that every enzyme in the 70 °C sample was permanently denatured.',A('Activity remains at 8 units, so some enzyme may remain functional; the experiment also does not establish that no recovery could occur over a longer period. Award one of these specific limitations.'))])
b.add('C1.1','A buffered pH investigation','A student compares catalase activity at pH 4, 6 and 8. They use equal volumes of crude potato extract but different hydrogen peroxide concentrations at each pH.',refs=[8,9],parts=[P('Explain why the investigation cannot isolate the effect of pH.',R('Substrate concentration also varies and can affect enzyme–substrate collision frequency and rate.'),R('The effects of pH and substrate concentration are confounded, so a rate difference cannot be attributed uniquely to pH.')),P('Give two specific improvements, each controlling a different variable.',A('Use the same final hydrogen peroxide concentration and reaction volume at all pH values.'),A('Maintain a fixed reaction temperature, for example with a thermostatically controlled water bath. Accept standardized enzyme concentration from one mixed extract.'))])
b.add('C1.1','Initial rate from product accumulation','The table gives constructed measurements for one enzyme assay. The initial interval is approximately linear.'+T(['Time / s','Product / μmol'],[[0,0],[10,3.6],[20,7.2],[40,12.0],[60,14.4]]),refs=[9,8],kind='data-based',diagram=graph([0,10,20,40,60],[('Product',[0,3.6,7.2,12,14.4],blue)],'Time / s','Product / μmol','Constructed enzyme product-time data',xlim=(0,60),ylim=(0,16),xticks=[0,20,40,60],yticks=[0,4,8,12,16]),parts=[P('Calculate the initial rate using the interval 0–20 s.',M('Use change in product divided by time: (7.2−0)/(20−0).'),A('0.36 μmol s⁻¹.')),P('Explain why the average rate over 0–60 s is lower than the initial rate.',R('Substrate is used up, reducing its concentration and the frequency of productive enzyme–substrate collisions. Accept product inhibition if explicitly presented as a possible explanation.')),P('Give one advantage of comparing initial rates in different substrate concentrations.',A('Initial rates are measured before substantial substrate depletion or product accumulation changes the starting conditions.'))])
b.calc('initial reaction rate','7.2/20',.36,'μmol s⁻¹')
b.add('C1.1','Two routes to the same products','A constructed energy profile gives the energy of substrates as 20 kJ mol⁻¹ and products as 5 kJ mol⁻¹. The peak is 75 kJ mol⁻¹ without enzyme and 40 kJ mol⁻¹ with enzyme.',refs=[10],parts=[P('Calculate the activation energy for each route.',A('Without enzyme: 75−20 = 55 kJ mol⁻¹.'),A('With enzyme: 40−20 = 20 kJ mol⁻¹.')),P('Explain whether adding enzyme changes the net energy released by the reaction.',A('Both routes release 20−5 = 15 kJ mol⁻¹.'),R('The enzyme changes the pathway and its activation barrier, but not the substrate or product energy levels.'))])
b.calc('uncatalysed activation energy','75-20',55,'kJ mol⁻¹');b.calc('catalysed activation energy','40-20',20,'kJ mol⁻¹');b.calc('energy released','20-5',15,'kJ mol⁻¹')
b.add('C1.1','Saturation at constant enzyme concentration','An assay has excess cofactor, a constant pH and a constant temperature. Increasing substrate concentration from 8 to 16 mmol dm⁻³ causes almost no change in initial rate. Which explanation is most appropriate?',refs=[8],difficulty='medium',options=['Most active sites are occupied for most of the time.','The enzyme has been consumed by the 8 mmol dm⁻³ substrate.','Every extra substrate molecule denatures one enzyme molecule.','The substrate concentration has stopped affecting the number of collisions because molecular motion has ceased.'],reason='At substrate saturation, the available enzyme active sites are already occupied for most of the time, so additional substrate produces little further increase in rate.')
b.add('C1.1','Where catalysis occurs','Pancreatic protease is secreted into the small intestine. A different enzyme catalyses a step of glycolysis in a pancreatic cell.',refs=[11],difficulty='easy',parts=[P('Classify each reaction as intracellular or extracellular and justify the classifications.',A('The protease reaction in the intestinal lumen is extracellular because it occurs outside cells.'),A('The glycolysis reaction is intracellular because it occurs within the cell’s cytoplasm.')),P('Explain why both reactions still depend on enzymes despite occurring in different locations.',R('Both require specific catalysts that lower activation barriers so biologically useful reaction rates are achieved.'))])
b.add('C1.1','Metabolism and body heat','A constructed energy budget for a resting mammal shows 500 kJ of chemical energy released from substrates over an interval. During that interval, 190 kJ is temporarily transferred into useful cellular chemical work; the remaining energy is measured as heat. Ignore other transfers.',refs=[12],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Calculate the energy transferred as heat and its percentage of the released energy.',A('500−190 = 310 kJ.'),M('Heat percentage = 310/500 ×100.'),A('62%.')),P('Explain one physiological benefit of this heat production for a mammal in a cold environment.',R('Metabolic heat helps replace heat lost to the environment, contributing to maintenance of a stable internal temperature.'))])
b.calc('metabolic heat','500-190',310,'kJ');b.calc('heat percentage','(500-190)/500*100',62,'%')
b.add('C1.1','Recognizing a metabolic cycle','Pathway X converts an initial substrate through several steps to a different final product. In pathway Y, a four-carbon acceptor combines with an incoming two-carbon group; later reactions regenerate the four-carbon acceptor.',refs=[13],parts=[P('Identify the pathway organization of X and Y and name a respiration example of each.',A('X is linear; glycolysis is an example.'),A('Y is cyclical; the Krebs cycle is an example.')),P('Explain why regeneration of the acceptor does not mean that the cycle needs no further inputs.',R('New incoming substrate is required on each turn and is converted to products; regeneration conserves the acceptor, not the incoming substrate.'))])
b.add('C1.1','Distinguishing reversible inhibitors','Constructed initial rates are shown for the same enzyme at fixed enzyme concentration. Inhibitors X and Y bind reversibly.'+T(['Substrate / mmol dm⁻³','No inhibitor','With X','With Y'],[[1,20,8,10],[5,60,35,30],[20,90,78,45],[100,99,97,49]]),refs=[14,15],kind='data-based',difficulty='hard',parts=[P('Identify which inhibitor is most consistent with competitive inhibition and justify your choice using the data.',A('X is most consistent with competitive inhibition.'),R('At high substrate concentration its rate approaches the uninhibited rate (97 versus 99), consistent with substrate outcompeting inhibitor at the active site.')),P('Explain the pattern for Y using an allosteric model.',R('Y binds to a separate allosteric site and changes the active-site conformation.'),R('Increasing substrate cannot displace Y from that site; the attainable rate remains lower.')),P('State why rates alone do not establish the exact molecular binding site.',A('Other molecular mechanisms can produce similar kinetic patterns; direct binding or structural evidence is needed to locate the site.'))])
b.add('C1.1','Statins and competition','A statin competes reversibly with a natural substrate for binding to the active site of an enzyme in cholesterol synthesis. The enzyme concentration remains fixed. Which change is most likely to reduce the percentage inhibition in an isolated assay?',refs=[15],difficulty='easy',options=['Increase the concentration of the natural substrate.','Remove all natural substrate.','Permanently destroy the enzyme’s active site.','Lower the enzyme concentration while leaving the inhibitor-to-substrate ratio unchanged.'],reason='A higher substrate concentration increases the chance that substrate occupies the shared active site rather than the reversible competitive inhibitor.')
b.add('C1.1','Isoleucine feedback','A simplified original model shows a pathway producing isoleucine from threonine. Isoleucine binds reversibly at a regulatory site of the enzyme catalysing the first step.',refs=[14,16],diagram=pathway(['Threonine','Intermediate 1','Intermediate 2','Isoleucine'],['E1','E2','E3'],title='Simplified feedback control of isoleucine synthesis',feedback='End product inhibits E1'),parts=[P('Explain how this arrangement avoids unnecessary accumulation of isoleucine.',R('As isoleucine accumulates, more binds to E1’s regulatory site and reduces its activity.'),R('The first committed reaction slows, reducing flux through the pathway and further isoleucine production.')),P('Predict the response when isoleucine is used rapidly in protein synthesis.',A('Its concentration falls and reversible inhibition is reduced.'),R('E1 activity and pathway flux increase, helping replenish isoleucine.'))])
b.add('C1.1','An altered penicillin target','A constructed experiment compares bacterial transpeptidase before and after exposure to penicillin. The original enzyme remains inactive after free penicillin is removed. A mutant enzyme catalyses cell-wall cross-linking but is much less affected by penicillin.',refs=[17],kind='data-based',difficulty='hard',parts=[P('Explain why removing free penicillin does not immediately restore the original enzyme’s activity.',R('Penicillin forms a persistent chemical attachment at the active site, causing mechanism-based irreversible inhibition.'),R('Removing unbound inhibitor does not reverse that chemical change to already inhibited enzyme molecules.')),P('Explain how the mutant enzyme can confer resistance without losing all of its normal function.',R('A change in active-site structure can reduce interaction or reaction with penicillin.'),R('The altered enzyme can still accept its natural substrate and catalyse sufficient cell-wall cross-linking.')),P('Explain why this result does not show that penicillin caused the mutation.',A('The experiment compares enzyme types but does not establish when or how the mutation arose; selection of pre-existing variants is an alternative.'))])

# C1.2: 017–032.
b.add('C1.2','ATP as a transferable energy supply','Which statement correctly links ATP with a cellular process?',refs=[1,2,3],difficulty='easy',options=['Hydrolysis of ATP can be coupled to active transport against a concentration gradient.','ATP is a protein whose amino acids are used to construct membrane pumps.','Diffusion down a concentration gradient always requires ATP hydrolysis.','ATP synthesis from ADP and phosphate releases the energy needed for all cellular work.'],reason='ATP is the nucleotide adenosine triphosphate. Its hydrolysis can supply energy through coupling to energy-requiring processes such as active transport; ATP synthesis requires an energy input.')
b.add('C1.2','The ATP–ADP cycle','A cell continually converts ADP and inorganic phosphate into ATP, then uses ATP for membrane transport and chromosome movement.',refs=[1,2,3],parts=[P('State the full name of ATP and identify its broad class of molecule.',A('Adenosine triphosphate.'),A('A nucleotide.')),P('Explain why ATP is suitable for distributing energy within the cell but is not an unlimited energy source.',R('ATP hydrolysis can be coupled to many cellular reactions or processes, transferring energy in usable amounts.'),R('ATP must be regenerated from ADP and phosphate using an energy supply, such as energy released during respiration.'))])
b.add('C1.2','Respiration is not ventilation','A student says, “When an animal stops ventilating its lungs for a few seconds, respiration immediately stops in every cell.”',refs=[4,5],parts=[P('Distinguish ventilation or gas exchange from cell respiration.',A('Ventilation moves air into and out of lungs; gas exchange moves gases across a respiratory surface. Accept either distinction matching the prompt.'),A('Cell respiration comprises chemical reactions in cells that release energy from carbon compounds to make ATP.')),P('Give two reasons why the student’s immediate-stop conclusion is not justified.',R('Oxygen already present in blood or tissues can support aerobic respiration briefly.'),R('Some cells can continue anaerobic respiration without an immediate external oxygen supply.'))])
b.add('C1.2','Respiratory choices in a muscle cell','A skeletal muscle cell can respire aerobically and can also convert glucose to lactate when oxygen supply is insufficient.',refs=[5],parts=[P('Write a simple word equation for aerobic respiration of glucose and one for the anaerobic pathway in this cell.',A('Glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water; energy release may be stated.'),A('Glucose → lactate; energy release may be stated. Do not accept ethanol as the human product.')),P('Compare ATP yield and cellular location of the two pathways.',A('Aerobic respiration yields substantially more ATP per glucose than the anaerobic pathway.'),A('Anaerobic reactions occur in the cytoplasm; completion of aerobic respiration requires mitochondria, although glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm.'))])
b.add('C1.2','A corrected respirometer reading','In a constructed respirometer experiment, carbon dioxide is absorbed. Over 12 minutes the apparatus containing 2.0 g of germinating seeds shows a gas-volume decrease of 0.96 cm³. A matched non-respiring control shows a decrease of 0.12 cm³ because of environmental changes.',refs=[6],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Calculate the corrected oxygen uptake per gram per minute.',M('Corrected volume = 0.96−0.12 = 0.84 cm³.'),M('Divide by 2.0 g and 12 min.'),A('0.035 cm³ g⁻¹ min⁻¹.')),P('Explain why carbon dioxide is absorbed in this method.',R('Otherwise carbon dioxide production can replace oxygen removed from the gas phase, masking the volume change due to oxygen uptake.')),P('State one condition that should be matched between the experimental and control apparatus.',A('The same temperature and initial gas volume. Award one condition; accept the same apparatus/capillary dimensions or matched displacement volume.'))])
b.calc('mass-specific oxygen uptake','(.96-.12)/(2.0*12)',.035,'cm³ g⁻¹ min⁻¹')
b.add('C1.2','Temperature and seed respiration','Constructed oxygen-uptake means were obtained from six independent groups of germinating seeds at each temperature.'+T(['Temperature / °C','Mean uptake / μmol g⁻¹ min⁻¹','SD'],[[10,1.2,.2],[20,2.4,.3],[30,4.1,.5],[40,2.8,1.1]]),refs=[6],kind='data-based',difficulty='hard',parts=[P('Describe the trend in the mean rates without assuming an exact optimum temperature.',A('Mean rate rises from 1.2 at 10 °C to 4.1 at 30 °C, then falls to 2.8 at 40 °C.'),A('The largest measured mean is at 30 °C, but the exact optimum may lie between tested temperatures.')),P('Interpret the larger SD at 40 °C.',A('The replicate rates at 40 °C are more dispersed around their mean; this does not by itself establish a statistically significant difference.')),P('Propose one additional measurement that would help determine whether the lower rate at 40 °C is reversible.',A('Return treated seeds to a standard lower temperature and measure whether their respiration recovers, using matched untreated controls.'))])
b.add('C1.2','Hydrogen transfer and oxidation','During a respiration step, hydrogen is removed from a carbon substrate and accepted by NAD. Which pair of changes is correct?',refs=[7],difficulty='easy',options=['The substrate is oxidized and NAD is reduced.','The substrate is reduced and NAD is oxidized.','Both the substrate and NAD are oxidized.','Neither molecule undergoes a redox change.'],reason='Removal of hydrogen with its electrons oxidizes the substrate; NAD gains reducing equivalents and becomes reduced NAD.')
b.add('C1.2','An ATP account for glycolysis','A constructed diagram of glycolysis records an investment of two ATP and a later production of four ATP for each glucose molecule. The six-carbon substrate is split into two three-carbon molecules before the later reactions.',refs=[8],diagram=pathway(['Glucose\n6 carbons','Two 3-carbon\nmolecules','Two pyruvate\nmolecules'],['2 ATP used','4 ATP produced'],title='Simplified glycolysis ATP accounting; intermediate names omitted'),parts=[P('Calculate the net ATP yield per glucose.',A('4−2 = 2 ATP molecules per glucose.')),P('Explain why lysis produces two molecules of pyruvate for each glucose.',R('One six-carbon molecule is split into two three-carbon molecules, each processed to pyruvate.')),P('State two other features of glycolysis represented incompletely by this simple diagram.',A('Oxidation transfers hydrogen/electrons to NAD, producing reduced NAD.'),A('Each step is catalysed by a specific enzyme. Accept initial phosphorylation activates the substrate, if distinct from the ATP calculation.'))])
b.calc('glycolysis net ATP','4-2',2,'ATP per glucose')
b.add('C1.2','Why lactate formation keeps glycolysis running','In an oxygen-limited muscle cell, a treatment prevents pyruvate from accepting hydrogen from reduced NAD. The treatment does not initially inhibit any glycolytic enzyme.',refs=[7,8,9],difficulty='hard',parts=[P('Explain why glycolysis eventually slows despite its enzymes remaining functional.',R('Reduced NAD is not efficiently reoxidized to NAD through lactate formation.'),R('The supply of oxidized NAD becomes limiting for the oxidation step of glycolysis.'),R('Glycolysis therefore slows and its substrate-level ATP production decreases.')),P('State the direct role of pyruvate-to-lactate conversion in the normal pathway.',A('It regenerates NAD by accepting hydrogen/electrons from reduced NAD; the conversion itself does not add an extra ATP yield.'))])
b.add('C1.2','Yeast in a sealed dough model','In a constructed low-oxygen dough experiment, yeast releases a gas that increases dough volume. A separate chemical assay detects ethanol.',refs=[10],parts=[P('Identify the gas and explain how the products support an anaerobic pathway in yeast.',A('The gas is carbon dioxide.'),R('Yeast anaerobic metabolism produces ethanol and carbon dioxide while regenerating NAD, allowing glycolysis to continue.')),P('Explain one similarity and one difference between this process and lactate formation in human muscle.',A('Both regenerate NAD so glycolysis can supply a small net ATP yield without oxygen.'),A('Yeast produces ethanol and carbon dioxide, whereas human muscle produces lactate without carbon dioxide release in this pathway.'))])
b.add('C1.2','Following carbon into the Krebs cycle','An original simplified carbon-flow model starts with one pyruvate molecule containing three carbon atoms. After the link reaction, its acetyl group is transferred to a four-carbon acceptor.',refs=[11,12],parts=[P('Account for the three carbon atoms during the link reaction.',A('One carbon is released as carbon dioxide by decarboxylation.'),A('The remaining two form an acetyl group carried by coenzyme A.')),P('Name the four-carbon acceptor and the six-carbon compound formed when it receives the acetyl group.',A('Oxaloacetate is the four-carbon acceptor.'),A('Citrate is the six-carbon product.')),P('State the redox change that accompanies the link reaction.',A('Pyruvate is oxidized by removal of hydrogen/electrons, reducing NAD.'))])
b.add('C1.2','A cycle that regenerates its acceptor','One turn of the Krebs cycle begins when a two-carbon acetyl group combines with oxaloacetate. The cycle releases two carbon dioxide molecules and regenerates oxaloacetate.',refs=[12],parts=[P('Explain why oxaloacetate need not be supplied afresh for every turn.',R('It is regenerated by the sequence of reactions and can accept another acetyl group.')),P('State two forms in which energy from substrate oxidation is transferred during the cycle.',A('Reduced electron/hydrogen carriers, including reduced NAD.'),A('ATP, or an equivalent nucleotide triphosphate that can transfer phosphate to ADP.')),P('Explain why the number of carbon dioxide molecules produced in one turn does not prove that both contain the newly entering acetyl carbons.',R('Carbon atoms mix within cycle intermediates; tracing particular atoms requires labelling or reaction-position evidence, not only a carbon count.'))])
b.add('C1.2','Electron transport and proton pumping','An isolated mitochondrial preparation has an intact inner membrane. Reduced NAD supplies electrons to a carrier chain. The chain pumps protons from the matrix to the intermembrane space.',refs=[13,14,15],diagram=SVG(txt(380,40,'Intermembrane space')+line(50,140,710,140,blue,7)+line(50,185,710,185,blue,7)+box(135,115,145,95,'')+box(485,115,145,95,'')+arrow(205,220,205,85,orange,3)+txt(245,85,'H⁺',18)+arrow(558,85,558,220,orange,3)+txt(595,85,'H⁺',18)+txt(205,260,'Electron carriers',16)+txt(558,260,'ATP synthase',16)+txt(380,320,'Matrix')+txt(380,365,'Schematic inner membrane; protein shapes are simplified.'),'Proton transfer across an original mitochondrial inner-membrane schematic'),parts=[P('Explain how reduced NAD helps establish the proton gradient.',R('It transfers electrons to the carrier chain and is oxidized back to NAD.'),R('Energy released during electron transfer drives proton pumping against the electrochemical gradient.')),P('Explain how the gradient contributes to ATP synthesis.',R('Protons return to the matrix through ATP synthase down their electrochemical gradient.'),R('ATP synthase couples this transfer to phosphorylation of ADP with inorganic phosphate.'))])
b.add('C1.2','Oxygen withdrawal from mitochondria','A constructed oxygen-electrode assay shows that ATP production falls rapidly when oxygen is exhausted, even though reduced NAD and ADP remain available.',refs=[13,14,15,16],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Explain why oxygen is necessary for continued electron flow.',R('Oxygen is the final electron acceptor in the respiratory chain.'),R('It combines with electrons and protons to form water, allowing upstream carriers to continue transferring electrons.')),P('Explain the resulting fall in ATP production when oxygen is exhausted.',R('Electron flow and associated proton pumping decline or stop.'),R('The proton gradient dissipates, reducing chemiosmotic ATP synthesis.'))])
b.add('C1.2','A membrane proton leak','A controlled mitochondrial experiment uses a compound that allows protons to cross the inner membrane without passing through ATP synthase. The electron transport chain remains functional and oxygen is present. Which immediate pattern is most consistent with the treatment?',refs=[14,15,16],difficulty='hard',options=['A smaller proton gradient and less ATP made per oxygen consumed.','A larger proton gradient and more ATP made per oxygen consumed.','ATP synthesis becomes independent of ADP availability.','Oxygen is no longer the terminal electron acceptor.'],reason='Protons bypass ATP synthase, dissipating part of the gradient without coupling that transfer to phosphorylation. Electron transport can continue, but ATP yield per oxygen consumed decreases.')
b.add('C1.2','Comparing respiratory fuels','A table gives constructed complete-oxidation energy yields for two pure substrates.'+T(['Substrate','Energy released / kJ g⁻¹'],[['Carbohydrate',16],['Lipid',37]]),refs=[17],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Calculate the mass of lipid that releases the same energy as 5.0 g of this carbohydrate.',M('Energy from carbohydrate = 5.0×16 = 80 kJ; divide by 37 kJ g⁻¹.'),A('2.2 g to two significant figures (unrounded 2.162 g).')),P('Explain why lipids generally yield more energy per gram when fully oxidized.',R('Lipids contain less oxygen and more oxidizable carbon and hydrogen relative to their mass.')),P('Explain why this does not make fatty acids a replacement for glucose in anaerobic glycolysis.',R('Fatty acids are broken down into acetyl groups entering aerobic pathways; they do not supply the glycolytic substrate needed for anaerobic ATP production.'))])
