b.add('C1.3','Changing the limiting factor','Constructed rates were measured at two light intensities and two carbon dioxide concentrations. Temperature and leaf area were kept constant.'+T(['Light / relative units','CO₂ / ppm','Net CO₂ uptake / μmol m⁻² s⁻¹'],[[100,400,4.0],[100,800,4.1],[600,400,10.0],[600,800,15.0]]),refs=[7],kind='data-based',difficulty='hard',parts=[P('Use the data to explain why carbon dioxide has a larger effect at the higher light intensity.',R('At low light, doubling carbon dioxide changes the rate only from 4.0 to 4.1, consistent with light limiting the rate.'),R('At high light, increased carbon dioxide raises the rate from 10.0 to 15.0, indicating that carbon dioxide supply had become more limiting.')),P('Calculate the percentage increase caused by added carbon dioxide at the higher light intensity.',M('(15.0−10.0)/10.0 ×100.'),A('50%.')),P('State why these four means alone do not establish statistical significance.',A('No replicate variation or uncertainty is supplied, so the reliability of the differences cannot be assessed statistically.'))])
b.calc('CO2 enrichment percentage increase','(15.0-10.0)/10.0*100',50,'%')
b.add('C1.3','A lamp-distance confound','A student moves a hot lamp closer to an aquatic plant and records more oxygen bubbles per minute. They conclude that increasing light intensity alone increased photosynthesis.',refs=[7],parts=[P('Identify a confounding variable and explain its effect on the conclusion.',A('Water temperature may rise as the hot lamp is moved closer.'),R('Temperature can change enzyme activity, so the observed change cannot be attributed only to light intensity.')),P('Give a specific way to control that confound and a better measure of oxygen production than bubble number.',A('Use a thermostatically controlled bath or heat filter and monitor water temperature throughout.'),A('Measure oxygen volume per unit time or dissolved oxygen change per unit time; bubbles need not have equal volumes.')),P('State one further control needed when comparing different plants.',A('Standardize photosynthetic tissue amount, for example exposed leaf area; accept species, age or carbon dioxide supply.'))])
b.add('C1.3','Field enrichment and greenhouse results','In two constructed studies, increasing carbon dioxide from 420 to 650 ppm increased mean dry mass by 28% in a well-watered greenhouse and 9% in an open-field enrichment experiment. Each percentage compares treatment with its own local control.',refs=[8],kind='data-based',difficulty='hard',parts=[P('Suggest two reasons why the field response might be smaller without contradicting a direct effect of carbon dioxide on photosynthesis.',R('Water, mineral nutrients or other resources may limit growth more strongly in the field.'),R('Variable weather, herbivory or competition can reduce the translation of increased photosynthesis into accumulated biomass. Award a distinct factor.')),P('Explain one strength of the open-field design and one strength of the greenhouse design.',A('Open-field enrichment retains more natural environmental and ecological conditions, improving relevance to field growth.'),A('A greenhouse can control variables more tightly, helping isolate the carbon dioxide treatment.')),P('Explain why the two percentages should not simply be subtracted to estimate an effect of drought.',A('The studies differ in multiple conditions and no isolated drought treatment is specified; a 19-percentage-point difference cannot uniquely be attributed to drought.'))])
b.add('C1.3','A photosystem is an organized array','A researcher compares isolated chlorophyll molecules with a functioning photosystem embedded in a membrane.',refs=[9,10],parts=[P('Explain two advantages of organizing different pigments in a photosystem.',R('Different accessory pigments absorb different wavelengths, broadening the usable range of light.'),R('Excitation energy can be transferred through the antenna array to a reaction-centre chlorophyll.')),P('Describe the event at the reaction centre that initiates electron transfer.',A('An excited electron is transferred from the reaction-centre chlorophyll to an acceptor, leaving the reaction centre oxidized.')),P('State one location of photosystems in a plant and one in a cyanobacterium.',A('Plant: thylakoid membranes within chloroplasts.'),A('Cyanobacterium: photosynthetic membranes, commonly internal thylakoid membranes; not chloroplasts.'))])
b.add('C1.3','Products of water splitting','At photosystem II, water molecules supply replacement electrons to an oxidized reaction centre.',refs=[11],difficulty='easy',parts=[P('State the three products formed when water is split in this process.',A('Electrons.'),A('Protons/hydrogen ions.'),A('Oxygen.')),P('Explain why the oxygen released is described as a by-product.',R('The light reactions require the electrons and contribute protons to the gradient; the released oxygen is not required as a product for carbon fixation. Accept that it can subsequently support respiration.'))])
b.add('C1.3','A thylakoid proton gradient','A constructed chloroplast preparation is illuminated. Electron transport increases the proton concentration in the thylakoid lumen relative to the stroma.',refs=[12,14],parts=[P('State the direction of proton movement through ATP synthase and explain how it is coupled to ATP production.',A('Protons move from the lumen to the stroma through ATP synthase.'),R('Movement down the electrochemical gradient drives the enzyme to phosphorylate ADP using inorganic phosphate.')),P('Predict the effect on ATP formation if the thylakoid membrane becomes freely permeable to protons.',A('ATP formation by chemiosmosis decreases.'),R('A proton gradient cannot be maintained because protons can leak across the membrane without passing through ATP synthase.'))])
b.add('C1.3','Cyclic and non-cyclic electron flow','An isolated photosynthetic membrane preparation produces ATP in light but no net reduced NADP and no oxygen. Which process is consistent with this combination?',refs=[12,13],difficulty='medium',options=['Cyclic electron flow involving photosystem I.','Net photolysis of water followed by reduction of NADP.','The Calvin cycle operating without any light reaction.','Complete oxidation of glucose in the thylakoid lumen.'],reason='Cyclic flow returns electrons from photosystem I to an electron-transfer pathway, supporting proton pumping and ATP formation without net NADP reduction or water splitting.')
b.add('C1.3','Tracing reducing power','A light-reaction model shows electrons leaving photosystem I and being transferred to NADP. The model uses the paired terms NADP and reduced NADP throughout.',refs=[13,14],parts=[P('State what NADP receives during reduction.',A('Two electrons and a hydrogen ion/proton, with the proton supplied from the stroma.')),P('Explain why excitation at photosystem I is needed even though the electrons were previously excited at photosystem II.',R('Electrons lose energy during transfers between the photosystems, including transfers coupled to proton pumping.'),R('Light absorbed by photosystem I raises their energy again, permitting transfer into reduced NADP.')),P('State one role of reduced NADP in the following stage of photosynthesis.',A('It supplies reducing power to convert glycerate 3-phosphate into triose phosphate in the Calvin cycle.'))])
b.add('C1.3','A carbon balance for fixation','Rubisco catalyses addition of carbon dioxide to RuBP. The short-lived product divides into two molecules of glycerate 3-phosphate.',refs=[15],diagram=pathway(['RuBP + CO₂','Short-lived\n6-carbon product','2 × glycerate\n3-phosphate'],['Rubisco','Splitting'],title='Simplified carbon fixation model with a transient product'),parts=[P('Deduce the number of carbon atoms in RuBP and justify your answer.',A('Five carbon atoms.'),R('The two three-carbon products contain six carbons in total, of which one entered as carbon dioxide.')),P('Explain why chloroplasts contain a high concentration of Rubisco.',R('Rubisco has a relatively low catalytic turnover, so many enzyme molecules are needed for a substantial fixation rate. Accept limited effectiveness at low carbon dioxide as additional context.')),P('State the chloroplast compartment where these reactions occur.',A('The stroma.'))])
b.add('C1.3','Reduction of a Calvin-cycle intermediate','A constructed chloroplast extract contains glycerate 3-phosphate (GP). In a complete mixture with ATP and reduced NADP it produces triose phosphate (TP). Removing either ATP or reduced NADP prevents sustained TP production.',refs=[16,19],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Explain the separate roles of ATP and reduced NADP.',A('ATP supplies energy through coupled reactions/phosphorylation.'),A('Reduced NADP supplies hydrogen/electrons that reduce the carbon intermediate.')),P('Explain why TP production depends indirectly on illumination in an intact chloroplast.',R('Light-dependent reactions regenerate ATP and reduced NADP.'),R('Without light, existing supplies become depleted and cannot sustain GP reduction.'))])
b.add('C1.3','Regenerating the carbon acceptor','A simplified Calvin-cycle account shows that six TP molecules, each with three carbons, are produced after fixation of three carbon dioxide molecules. Five of the six TP molecules are used to regenerate RuBP.',refs=[17],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Use a carbon balance to calculate how many RuBP molecules can be regenerated.',M('Five TP molecules contain 5×3 = 15 carbons.'),A('15/5 = 3 RuBP molecules.')),P('Calculate the number of carbon dioxide molecules that must be fixed to provide a net six-carbon carbohydrate, assuming this accounting applies and no carbon is lost.',A('Six carbon dioxide molecules; two sets of three produce two net TP molecules containing six carbons.')),P('Explain the consequence of stopping RuBP regeneration.',R('The available carbon dioxide acceptor is depleted, so Rubisco-mediated fixation cannot continue even if carbon dioxide remains present.'))])
b.calc('regenerated RuBP','5*3/5',3,'molecules');b.calc('CO2 per net six-carbon carbohydrate','3*2',6,'molecules')
b.add('C1.3','Making nitrogen-containing compounds','A plant receives ample light, water and carbon dioxide but very little available nitrogen. It continues to make some carbohydrate but its protein synthesis and growth slow.',refs=[18],parts=[P('Explain how carbon fixed in photosynthesis can contribute to proteins.',R('Calvin-cycle products provide carbon skeletons that can be converted into amino acids.'),R('Amino acids are assembled into proteins; mineral nitrogen supplies the required nitrogen atoms.')),P('Explain why more carbon dioxide alone may fail to restore normal protein synthesis.',R('Nitrogen remains limiting; additional carbon skeletons cannot replace the nitrogen needed to make amino acids.')),P('State one other class of carbon compound that can be synthesized from photosynthetic products.',A('Lipids, nucleic acids or another named carbon compound with an appropriate metabolic link.'))])
b.add('C1.3','When carbon fixation is blocked','An illuminated, intact chloroplast is suddenly deprived of carbon dioxide. At first, light-driven electron flow continues, but its rate later falls. Assume no alternative sustained electron sink.',refs=[19,13,16],difficulty='hard',parts=[P('Explain why the immediate and later responses differ.',R('Existing Calvin-cycle intermediates and oxidized NADP permit a brief continuation of reactions.'),R('Carbon fixation then declines, reducing use of ATP and reduced NADP.'),R('Oxidized NADP becomes less available as a terminal acceptor for non-cyclic electron flow, so that flow slows.')),P('Explain why the term “light-independent” does not mean that carbon fixation is indefinitely independent of light.',R('The Calvin cycle requires ATP and reduced NADP supplied by light-dependent reactions, so it cannot continue indefinitely when those supplies cease.'))])

# C2.1: 049–064. Every assessed statement in this topic is AHL.
b.add('C2.1','A signal reaches many cells','A protein hormone circulates past several cell types, but only one type changes its metabolism. Which explanation best accounts for the selectivity?',refs=[1,7],difficulty='easy',options=['The responding cells possess a suitable receptor and functional signalling pathway.','All other cells must be isolated from the blood.','All exposed cells bind the hormone equally, but only the responding type receives any oxygen.','The responding cells convert the hormone directly into a nutrient used by every metabolic pathway.'],reason='A ligand has specific receptor interactions; a cell’s receptor expression and downstream machinery determine whether it responds despite broad hormone exposure.')
b.add('C2.1','Density-dependent bacterial light','Constructed cultures of Vibrio fischeri are grown in identical nutrient conditions. At low cell density there is little light. At high density light production rises. Cell-free medium from a high-density culture triggers light production when added to a low-density culture.',refs=[2],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Explain these observations using quorum sensing.',R('Bacteria release a diffusible signalling molecule that accumulates as population density increases.'),R('Once its concentration reaches a response threshold, cells alter gene expression and produce bioluminescence.')),P('Explain why the cell-free medium result is stronger evidence than a density–light correlation alone.',R('It shows that a transferable extracellular factor can trigger the response without increasing the recipient culture’s cell density.')),P('Give one control needed when transferring the medium.',A('Transfer an equal volume of identically processed low-density/unconditioned medium, controlling nutrients, pH and dilution as appropriate.'))])
b.add('C2.1','Signals act at different scales','Consider four signalling events: insulin travels through blood to a muscle cell; acetylcholine crosses a neuromuscular synaptic cleft; a rise in cytoplasmic calcium triggers vesicle fusion; an immune cell releases a cytokine that changes another immune cell’s activity.',refs=[3,5],parts=[P('Classify the signalling chemical in each of the first three examples by its functional category.',A('Insulin is a hormone.'),A('Acetylcholine is a neurotransmitter.'),A('Calcium ions are intracellular signalling chemicals/second messengers in this example.')),P('Explain what the cytokine example shows about communication between immune cells, then compare the transport distance of the first two examples.',A('Cytokines act as signals between cells, coordinating immune-cell activity rather than acting as antibodies.'),A('A circulating hormone can act at distant target cells, whereas acetylcholine acts locally by diffusion across the short synaptic cleft.'))])
b.add('C2.1','Chemical diversity of signals','Insulin is a protein hormone, epinephrine is an amine and testosterone is a steroid. These compounds all carry information between cells but have different chemical properties.',refs=[4,6],parts=[P('Explain why a shared signalling function does not require the same chemical structure.',R('Information is conveyed by specific ligand–receptor interactions, so different molecular structures can each activate a matching receptor.')),P('Predict which of the listed signals is most able to cross a lipid bilayer directly and explain why.',A('Testosterone.'),R('Its steroid structure is largely hydrophobic/lipid-soluble, whereas the protein hormone cannot readily pass through the membrane’s hydrophobic interior.')),P('State one implication of this difference for receptor location.',A('Testosterone can bind an intracellular receptor; insulin normally binds a receptor exposed at the cell surface.'))])
b.add('C2.1','Two receptor locations','A receptor protein spans a plasma membrane. Its ligand-binding domain faces the extracellular fluid, while another domain projects into the cytoplasm.',refs=[6],parts=[P('Describe the expected distribution of hydrophobic and hydrophilic amino acids in this receptor.',A('The membrane-spanning surface has mainly hydrophobic residues that interact with the bilayer’s non-polar interior.'),A('Domains exposed to aqueous extracellular fluid or cytoplasm have hydrophilic surfaces.')),P('Explain how a ligand that remains outside the cell can still cause an intracellular response.',R('Ligand binding changes the receptor’s conformation or activity.'),R('The intracellular domain or associated proteins initiate a signalling pathway across the membrane.'))])
b.add('C2.1','Testing the order of a pathway','In a constructed cell model, ligand L binds receptor R, which activates protein G and then enzyme E. E produces messenger M, causing response Z. A receptor blocker prevents response Z to L, but supplying a membrane-permeable form of M restores Z.',refs=[7,9,10],diagram=pathway(['L binds R','G activates E','M increases','Response Z'],title='Original simplified receptor-to-response pathway'),kind='data-based',difficulty='hard',parts=[P('Explain what the rescue by M indicates about the site of the receptor blocker’s effect.',R('The response machinery downstream of M remains functional.'),R('The block lies at or before formation of M, consistent with inhibition of the receptor step.')),P('Explain why the rescue does not by itself prove that the blocker binds directly to R.',A('Blocking another upstream component, such as G or E, could also be bypassed by supplying M; direct receptor-binding evidence is needed.')),P('Name the second messenger used in the epinephrine pathway represented by this model.',A('Cyclic AMP/cAMP.'))])
b.add('C2.1','A ligand-gated cation channel','At a neuromuscular junction, acetylcholine binds to a receptor that also acts as an ion channel. At the resting membrane potential, opening the channel produces a net inward movement of positive charge.',refs=[8],parts=[P('Explain how receptor binding changes the postsynaptic membrane potential.',R('Acetylcholine binding opens the receptor’s channel.'),R('Net entry of cations makes the inside less negative, producing depolarization.')),P('Predict the effect of a competitive antagonist that occupies the acetylcholine-binding site without opening the channel.',A('It reduces channel opening and the postsynaptic depolarization produced by acetylcholine.')),P('Explain why this response can be rapid.',A('Binding directly changes an existing channel’s activity; synthesis of new proteins is not required.'))])
b.add('C2.1','A G protein relay','A ligand-bound membrane receptor activates an intracellular G protein. The ligand never enters the cytoplasm. Which statement best describes the receptor’s role?',refs=[9],difficulty='easy',options=['It conveys an extracellular binding event into activation of an intracellular relay.','It must move the ligand into the cytoplasm before the G protein can respond.','It acts only as an anchor and has no effect on the G protein after ligand binding.','It phosphorylates DNA directly, so no intracellular relay is needed.'],reason='A G-protein-coupled receptor changes activity after ligand binding and activates a cytoplasmic G protein, linking extracellular information to intracellular responses.')
b.add('C2.1','Amplification of an epinephrine signal','A constructed liver-cell model gives the following observations after an epinephrine pulse: activated receptors are followed by activated G proteins, then increased cAMP, then increased glycogen breakdown. Many cAMP molecules are produced for each activated receptor.',refs=[10],parts=[P('Identify the first messenger and second messenger.',A('Epinephrine/adrenaline is the first messenger.'),A('cAMP is the second messenger.')),P('Explain the advantage of producing many second-messenger molecules per activated receptor.',R('The signal is amplified, allowing a small extracellular hormone input to produce a substantial intracellular response.')),P('Explain why cAMP should subsequently be removed or inactivated.',R('Ending the intracellular signal permits the response to stop or adjust when the original hormone signal declines.'))])
b.add('C2.1','Insulin and transporter recruitment','Constructed measurements in skeletal muscle cells show the number of glucose transporters at the plasma membrane before and after insulin. Total cellular transporter number remains constant.'+T(['Time after insulin / min','Surface transporters / relative units'],[[0,20],[2,45],[5,75]]),refs=[11],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Explain how insulin can increase surface transporter number without making new transporter proteins.',R('Insulin binds its membrane receptor and stimulates intracellular tyrosine phosphorylation and a signalling cascade.'),R('Vesicles containing glucose transporters move to and fuse with the plasma membrane, inserting existing transporters.')),P('Calculate the percentage increase in surface transporters between 0 and 5 minutes.',M('(75−20)/20 ×100.'),A('275%.')),P('Explain why “275% increase” is not the same as “275% of the initial number”.',A('The final value is 375% of the initial number; percentage increase excludes the starting 100%.'))])
b.calc('surface transporter percentage increase','(75-20)/20*100',275,'%')
b.add('C2.1','Blocking receptor phosphorylation','In a constructed muscle-cell experiment, a drug prevents tyrosine phosphorylation by the insulin receptor but does not prevent insulin binding. Cells retain normal ATP concentration and an unchanged pool of transporter-containing vesicles.',refs=[11],kind='data-based',difficulty='hard',parts=[P('Predict the effect on insulin-stimulated glucose-transporter insertion and explain your prediction.',A('Insulin-stimulated insertion decreases.'),R('Ligand binding is insufficient when the receptor cannot initiate the downstream phosphorylation signal that recruits vesicles.')),P('Explain why the stated ATP and vesicle observations strengthen the interpretation.',R('They reduce alternative explanations based on a general energy failure or absence of available transporters.')),P('State one useful negative control.',A('Otherwise identical cells exposed to the drug solvent without the active drug.'))])
b.add('C2.1','Steroid regulation of transcription','A steroid hormone enters a target cell and binds an intracellular receptor. The activated complex associates with a particular DNA sequence.',refs=[12],diagram=pathway(['Steroid enters','Receptor\nactivated','DNA sequence\nbound','Transcription\nchanges'],title='Original simplified intracellular steroid-receptor model'),parts=[P('Explain how this sequence can alter the cell’s phenotype.',R('The activated receptor affects transcription of particular genes.'),R('The resulting changes in RNA and protein production alter cellular structure or activity.')),P('Explain why the same circulating steroid need not have identical effects in all cell types.',R('Cell types differ in receptor availability, accessible target genes and other regulatory proteins; award one well-developed factor.')),P('Name one steroid hormone that can use an intracellular receptor.',A('Testosterone, oestradiol/estradiol or progesterone.'))])
b.add('C2.1','Oestradiol and hypothalamic cells','In a constructed culture of hypothalamic cells that secrete gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH), oestradiol changes the transcription of a target gene. Cells lacking the oestradiol receptor show no such response.',refs=[12,13],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Explain the different responses of the two cell groups.',R('Oestradiol can activate its intracellular receptor in receptor-containing cells.'),R('The activated receptor regulates gene transcription; receptor-deficient cells cannot initiate that receptor-dependent pathway.')),P('State why this experiment cannot establish the direction of oestradiol’s feedback on GnRH secretion in an intact person.',A('No secretion result or whole-organism hormonal context is supplied; transcription of an unspecified target is insufficient to infer increased or decreased GnRH release.')),P('Suggest one measurement that would directly test the secretion response in this culture.',A('Measure GnRH released into the medium over a fixed interval, with an appropriate untreated control.'))])
b.add('C2.1','Progesterone-responsive endometrium','A constructed endometrial cell culture responds to progesterone by changing transcription and increasing secretion of a measured protein. An inhibitor of RNA synthesis prevents the later increase in secretion.',refs=[12,13],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Explain why this result is consistent with an intracellular receptor mechanism.',R('Progesterone binds an intracellular receptor that influences target-gene transcription.'),R('New RNA production is needed for the subsequent increase in protein synthesis/secretion, so blocking transcription can prevent the response.')),P('Explain why the result does not show that progesterone directly binds the DNA.',A('The hormone binds its receptor; the activated receptor interacts with DNA. The experiment provides no evidence of direct hormone–DNA binding.')),P('State the physiological tissue represented by the culture.',A('The uterine lining/endometrium.'))])
b.add('C2.1','Identifying feedback direction','Two constructed control loops are described. In loop X, a rise in a variable stimulates a response that lowers that variable. In loop Y, a stimulus produces a response that increases the original stimulus until an external endpoint is reached. Which option correctly identifies the two feedback loops?',refs=[14],difficulty='easy',options=['X is negative feedback; Y is positive feedback.','X is positive feedback; Y is negative feedback.','Both are negative feedback because each contains a response.','Both are positive feedback because signals move through the loops.'],reason='Negative feedback opposes an initial change, whereas positive feedback reinforces it. The terms describe the direction of the response, not whether its outcome is beneficial.')
b.add('C2.1','A self-amplifying signalling loop','In a simplified childbirth model, stretching of the cervix stimulates oxytocin release. Oxytocin promotes uterine contractions, which increase cervical stretching until delivery removes the stimulus.',refs=[14],parts=[P('Explain why the loop is positive feedback.',R('The response to stretching increases contractions, which produce more stretching.'),R('This reinforces the original stimulus and can amplify further oxytocin release.')),P('Explain why positive feedback requires an endpoint or limiting process.',R('Without a limiting process the response would continue to amplify; delivery removes the initiating stretch and ends this loop.')),P('Contrast this with one negative-feedback example.',A('For example, a rise in blood glucose stimulates insulin action that lowers blood glucose, opposing the initial rise. Accept another correctly explained negative-feedback loop.'))])

# C2.2: 065–080.
