b.add('C2.2','Recognizing neuron organization','The original schematic shows a neuron. X points to a region of short branched fibres; Y points to the long single fibre.',refs=[1],diagram=neuron,difficulty='easy',parts=[P('Identify X and Y.',A('X: dendrites.'),A('Y: axon.')),P('State two cellular features of the cell body.',A('It contains cytoplasm.'),A('It contains the nucleus.')),P('State the general information-carrying role of nerve fibres.',A('They conduct electrical impulses/action potentials between regions of the neuron.'))])
b.add('C2.2','Maintaining a resting potential','A resting neuron has a negative potential inside relative to outside. Its sodium–potassium pumps use ATP to transport sodium out and potassium in, while the membrane is more permeable to potassium than sodium.',refs=[2],parts=[P('Explain why maintaining ion gradients requires ATP.',R('The pump transports ions against their electrochemical gradients, requiring energy from ATP hydrolysis.')),P('Explain how selective permeability contributes to the negative internal potential.',R('Potassium tends to diffuse out down its concentration gradient through leak pathways.'),R('This leaves the inside relatively negative as large intracellular anions do not leave equally readily; the electrical gradient opposes further potassium loss.')),P('Predict the long-term consequence of stopping the pump.',A('The ion gradients and resting potential gradually diminish as passive ion movements continue. Do not require an instantaneous loss.'))])
b.add('C2.2','The electrical nature of an impulse','Which statement most accurately describes the electrical signal in an axon?',refs=[3],difficulty='easy',options=['A propagating change in membrane potential results from movement of ions.','A continuous stream of electrons travels through a metal-like axon core.','The sodium–potassium pump produces each rapid depolarization by reversing its normal transport direction.','The same small group of sodium ions travels the whole axon length without local membrane exchange.'],reason='Nerve impulses are propagated changes in membrane potential involving charged ions; the axon is not a metallic electrical wire.')
b.add('C2.2','Axon diameter and conduction speed','In a constructed comparative study of unmyelinated axons, diameter and conduction speed have a Pearson correlation coefficient r = +0.90. A linear model with an intercept is fitted to these data.',refs=[4],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Describe the direction and strength of the association.',A('A strong positive correlation: larger diameters tend to be associated with higher conduction speeds.')),P('Calculate R² and interpret it in this model.',A('R² = 0.90² = 0.81.'),R('About 81% of the observed variation in speed is accounted for by the fitted linear relationship with diameter; this is not a statement that diameter causes exactly 81% of speed.')),P('Explain why adding myelinated fibres without identifying them could complicate the interpretation.',R('Myelination also affects speed and could confound the apparent relationship between diameter and speed.'))])
b.calc('coefficient of determination','.90*.90',.81,'dimensionless')
b.add('C2.2','Direction at a chemical synapse','A typical chemical synapse has neurotransmitter-containing vesicles concentrated in one terminal and matching receptors on the opposing membrane.',refs=[5],parts=[P('Explain why transmission is normally one-way across this synapse.',R('Neurotransmitter is released from the presynaptic terminal.'),R('Appropriate receptors are located on the postsynaptic membrane, so the structural arrangement gives the signal its direction.')),P('State two possible types of postsynaptic cell.',A('Another neuron.'),A('An effector cell, such as a muscle or gland cell.'))])
b.add('C2.2','Calcium and vesicle release','In a constructed experiment, a presynaptic terminal is depolarized either in normal solution or in solution with very little extracellular calcium. Neurotransmitter release is high in normal solution and low in low-calcium solution.',refs=[6],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Explain the role of calcium in the normal response.',R('Depolarization opens calcium-permeable channels, allowing calcium ions to enter the terminal.'),R('The rise in intracellular calcium acts as a signal that triggers synaptic-vesicle fusion and neurotransmitter release.')),P('Explain why the experiment should confirm that the terminal depolarizes similarly in both solutions.',R('Otherwise reduced release could result from failure to depolarize rather than from the changed calcium supply.')),P('State one process by which vesicle contents enter the synaptic cleft.',A('Exocytosis.'))])
b.add('C2.2','After acetylcholine crosses the cleft','At an excitatory cholinergic synapse, acetylcholine diffuses across the cleft. What is its next direct action on the postsynaptic cell?',refs=[7],difficulty='easy',options=['It binds to membrane receptors and changes channel activity, producing depolarization.','It is first converted by acetylcholinesterase into the substance that activates the receptor.','It directly supplies ATP to power the postsynaptic sodium–potassium pump.','It must activate transcription in the postsynaptic nucleus before any membrane response occurs.'],reason='Acetylcholine binds to specific transmembrane receptors. At this excitatory synapse, the resulting channel opening produces a net depolarizing ionic current.')
