b.add('C2.2','Interpreting an action-potential trace','The constructed trace shows membrane potential inside an axon relative to outside. In this model, threshold is −55 mV.',refs=[8,10],kind='data-based',diagram=graph(apx,[('Membrane potential',apy,blue)],'Time / ms','Membrane potential / mV','Constructed single action-potential trace',xlim=(0,6),ylim=(-90,40),xticks=[0,1,2,3,4,5,6],yticks=[-80,-55,0,30],note='Constructed trace; threshold is supplied in the question.'),parts=[P('Explain the rapid rise from −55 mV to positive potential.',R('Threshold opens voltage-gated sodium channels.'),R('Sodium ions enter down their electrochemical gradient, depolarizing the membrane further.')),P('Explain the subsequent repolarization.',R('Sodium channels inactivate and voltage-gated potassium channels are open.'),R('Potassium efflux makes the inside negative again.')),P('Explain the brief potential below the resting value.',A('Potassium conductance remains elevated briefly as potassium channels close slowly, producing after-hyperpolarization.'))])
b.add('C2.2','Regenerating the impulse along an axon','One small region of an unmyelinated axon depolarizes during an action potential. The adjacent resting region is initially below threshold.',refs=[9],parts=[P('Explain how activity in the first region can trigger an action potential in the adjacent region.',R('Local ionic currents spread charge from the depolarized region into neighbouring membrane regions.'),R('The adjacent region is depolarized to threshold, opening its voltage-gated sodium channels and regenerating an action potential.')),P('Explain why the action potential is not merely a single passive voltage disturbance fading along the whole axon.',R('New action potentials are regenerated in successive membrane regions, maintaining the signal’s amplitude rather than relying only on passive spread.'))])
nodes=SVG(line(65,185,695,185,blue,5)+''.join(f'<rect x="{x}" y="155" width="145" height="60" rx="24" fill="#deebf1" stroke="#527188" stroke-width="2"/>' for x in [90,295,500])+txt(260,110,'Node',16)+arrow(260,120,265,175)+txt(165,270,'Myelin sheath',16)+arrow(165,250,165,220)+arrow(265,85,470,85,orange,3)+txt(370,58,'Local current spreads',15)+txt(380,345,'Original axon schematic; longitudinal distances are not to scale.'),'Original myelinated-axon schematic with gaps between sheaths')
b.add('C2.2','Myelin and nodal regeneration','The original schematic shows part of a myelinated axon. Ion channels that regenerate action potentials are concentrated at the gaps between myelin segments.',refs=[11],diagram=nodes,parts=[P('Name the gaps and explain their role in saltatory conduction.',A('Nodes of Ranvier.'),R('Local currents spread beneath the insulating myelin and bring the next node to threshold, where an action potential is regenerated.')),P('Explain why this arrangement can conduct faster than a comparable unmyelinated axon.',R('Action potentials need not be regenerated continuously along every small segment; myelin reduces current leakage and permits spread between nodes.')),P('Predict one effect of loss of myelin.',A('Conduction can slow or fail because current leaks before the next node reaches threshold.'))])
b.add('C2.2','Blocking neurotransmitter reuptake','In a constructed synapse model, a transporter normally removes a neurotransmitter from the cleft by returning it to the presynaptic cell. A drug such as cocaine inhibits this transporter.',refs=[12],parts=[P('Predict the immediate effect on neurotransmitter presence in the cleft.',A('The neurotransmitter remains in the cleft at a higher concentration or for longer after release.')),P('Explain how this can alter postsynaptic signalling.',R('Receptors can remain activated for longer or be activated more strongly, prolonging or increasing the response.')),P('Explain why blocking reuptake is not the same molecular action as blocking postsynaptic receptors.',R('Reuptake inhibition slows transmitter clearance; receptor antagonism prevents receptor activation despite transmitter presence.'))])
b.add('C2.2','Persistent receptor activation in an insect','A constructed insect-neuron assay tests a neonicotinoid that activates nicotinic acetylcholine receptors and is not removed rapidly by acetylcholinesterase. Initially the membrane depolarizes; later normal patterned impulses fail.',refs=[12],kind='data-based',difficulty='hard',parts=[P('Explain how a chemical that activates a receptor can ultimately disrupt transmission.',R('Persistent receptor activation can keep the postsynaptic membrane depolarized.'),R('Normal recovery and repeated action-potential generation are disrupted, so sustained depolarization can block normal signalling.')),P('Explain why describing every neonicotinoid simply as a competitive antagonist would be inaccurate.',A('The stated compound activates the receptor rather than merely occupying it without activation; the later functional block results from persistent stimulation.')),P('State one measurement that would distinguish the initial excitation from the later failure.',A('Record membrane potential or action-potential frequency over time after exposure, using an untreated control.'))])
b.add('C2.2','Combining excitatory and inhibitory inputs','In a supplied simplified model, a neuron rests at −70 mV and reaches threshold at −55 mV. Two simultaneous excitatory inputs each change potential by +8 mV; one simultaneous inhibitory input changes it by −6 mV. Assume these small changes add linearly for this calculation.',refs=[13,14],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Calculate the resulting potential with the two excitatory inputs alone and state whether threshold is reached.',A('−70 +8 +8 = −54 mV.'),A('Threshold is reached, so an action potential can be initiated.')),P('Calculate the potential when the inhibitory input is also present and predict the outcome.',A('−70 +8 +8−6 = −60 mV.'),A('Threshold is not reached, so no action potential is initiated in this model.')),P('Explain why the inhibitory input does not make the next action potential “partly smaller”.',R('The inputs affect whether threshold is reached; an action potential, once triggered, is an all-or-nothing event.'))])
b.calc('excitatory sum','-70+8+8',-54,'mV');b.calc('mixed-input sum','-70+8+8-6',-60,'mV')
b.add('C2.2','Capsaicin without high temperature','Capsaicin can open cation channels in certain free nerve endings in skin even when the skin is not unusually hot.',refs=[15],parts=[P('Explain how opening these channels can produce a pain signal.',R('Entry of positive ions depolarizes the sensory ending.'),R('If threshold is reached, action potentials propagate along the sensory neuron towards the central nervous system.'),R('Processing in the brain produces the perception of pain.')),P('Explain why the perception of burning does not prove that the tissue temperature rose.',A('A chemical can activate the sensory pathway normally stimulated by damaging heat; perception identifies pathway activation, not uniquely the stimulus type.'))])
b.add('C2.2','Consciousness and interacting networks','A neuroscientist describes consciousness as an emergent property of networks of interacting neurons. Which statement is most consistent with this description?',refs=[16],difficulty='medium',options=['Network interactions can give rise to properties not present in a single isolated neuron.','Every isolated neuron must have the same conscious experience as a person.','Consciousness can be completely inferred from the diameter of one axon.','Calling a property emergent means it cannot be investigated scientifically.'],reason='Emergence describes a property arising from interactions among components. It neither assigns a person’s experience to individual neurons nor rules out evidence-based investigation.')
b.add('C2.2','Frequency rather than pulse height','Constructed recordings from one sensory axon contain 12 action potentials during a 0.40 s weak stimulus and 28 during a 0.40 s stronger stimulus. Individual action-potential amplitudes are similar.',refs=[10,14],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Calculate action-potential frequency for each stimulus.',A('Weak: 12/0.40 = 30 Hz.'),A('Stronger: 28/0.40 = 70 Hz.')),P('Explain how the recording distinguishes signal frequency from action-potential amplitude.',R('The stronger stimulus is associated with more impulses per second, while each impulse remains approximately the same amplitude.')),P('State one limitation of drawing a general quantitative stimulus–frequency relationship from these data.',A('Only two stimulus levels or one axon are represented; repeated measurements across more levels/axons are needed to characterize the relationship.'))])
b.calc('weak stimulus frequency','12/.40',30,'Hz');b.calc('strong stimulus frequency','28/.40',70,'Hz')

# C3.1: 081–096.
b.add('C3.1','An integrated pursuit','During a short pursuit, a predator detects moving prey, changes direction, increases muscle activity and delivers more oxygen and fuel to its muscles.',refs=[1,2,3],parts=[P('Explain how two signalling systems and one transport system contribute to this response.',A('The nervous system rapidly conveys sensory information and coordinates motor output.'),A('The endocrine system releases hormones that coordinate changes across target organs.'),A('The blood system transports oxygen and metabolic substrates to active muscles and removes products.')),P('Explain why successful pursuit is an emergent property of the organism.',R('It depends on coordination among sensory, nervous, muscular and transport components; no isolated cell or organ performs the whole behaviour.'))])
b.add('C3.1','Combining present input with past experience','A person recognizes a familiar route by combining visual landmarks, sounds and remembered directions.',refs=[4],difficulty='easy',parts=[P('Explain the brain’s integrative role in this example.',R('It combines information arriving through different sensory inputs.'),R('It uses stored information from learning and memory to interpret the inputs and guide behaviour.')),P('Explain why the output cannot be described as a response to a single visual receptor alone.',A('The behaviour uses multiple sensory signals and past experience, requiring processing beyond one receptor.'))])
b.add('C3.1','A withdrawal circuit','An original simplified pain-reflex model is shown. The sensory receptor is a free nerve ending in the hand; one interneuron lies in the spinal cord’s grey matter.',refs=[5,6,7,9],diagram=pathway(['Sensory neuron\n(free ending)','Interneuron\n(spinal cord)','Motor neuron','Skeletal muscle'],title='Simplified withdrawal-reflex neural route; the effector is described in the question'),parts=[P('State the effector and explain its response.',A('A skeletal muscle, such as a flexor, is the effector.'),A('Its contraction withdraws the hand from the harmful stimulus.')),P('Explain why withdrawal can start before conscious awareness of pain.',R('The spinal cord can integrate this reflex locally and activate motor output without first requiring a conscious decision by the cerebral hemispheres.')),P('Distinguish the directions of information flow in the sensory and motor neurons.',A('Sensory neurons carry information towards the CNS; motor neurons carry output from the CNS to the effector.'))])
