b.add('C3.1','A nerve contains many fibres','The original cross-section shows a simplified nerve. X points to its outer protective layer, Y to a fibre surrounded by a thick ring, and Z to a small fibre without such a ring.',refs=[8],diagram=SVG(nervebody,'Original transverse nerve schematic with myelinated and unmyelinated fibres',760,400),parts=[P('Identify X and the feature distinguishing Y from Z.',A('X is the protective connective-tissue sheath of the nerve.'),A('Y is myelinated, while Z is unmyelinated.')),P('Explain why a nerve is not the same structure as a single neuron.',R('A nerve is a bundle of many nerve fibres/axons, whereas a neuron is one cell.')),P('State the two functional directions of signalling that can occur in a mixed nerve.',A('Sensory fibres carry input towards the CNS; motor fibres carry output towards effectors.'))])
b.add('C3.1','Coordinating movement','A person can initiate muscle contraction but has difficulty coordinating smooth movements and maintaining balance. Which brain region’s normal role is most directly related to these two functions?',refs=[10],difficulty='easy',options=['Cerebellum.','Pineal gland.','Hypothalamus.','Medulla oblongata.'],reason='The cerebellum contributes to coordination of skeletal-muscle activity and balance. This is a functional question, not sufficient evidence for a clinical diagnosis.')
b.add('C3.1','A daily melatonin pattern','Constructed measurements from one person under a regular light–dark schedule show low melatonin concentrations in daytime, rising concentrations after dark and a fall towards morning.',refs=[11],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Name the gland that secretes melatonin and explain its role in the sleep–wake cycle.',A('The pineal gland.'),R('Its daily secretion pattern signals biological night and helps regulate circadian sleep–wake timing.')),P('Explain why a single high melatonin measurement cannot by itself prove that the person is asleep.',A('Melatonin contributes to timing but sleep also depends on other factors; secretion and sleep state are not identical variables.')),P('Suggest one improvement for establishing a repeatable daily rhythm.',A('Sample at multiple defined times over several days under a documented light schedule, with repeated measurements.'))])
b.add('C3.1','Preparing for vigorous activity','Epinephrine is released during preparation for vigorous activity.',refs=[12],parts=[P('Name its source and describe two changes that help support active skeletal muscles.',A('The adrenal glands/adrenal medulla.'),A('Heart rate or force of contraction increases, improving blood delivery to muscles.'),A('Availability of metabolic fuel increases, for example through glycogen breakdown; accept a distinct relevant respiratory or blood-flow change.')),P('Explain why a blood-borne signal can coordinate several organs at once.',R('Circulation distributes the hormone to multiple organs; cells with appropriate receptors can respond simultaneously.'))])
b.add('C3.1','Linking the brain to endocrine glands','A simplified control pathway runs from the hypothalamus to the pituitary gland and then to another endocrine gland.',refs=[13],diagram=pathway(['Hypothalamus','Pituitary','Endocrine gland','Target tissue'],title='General hypothalamic–pituitary endocrine coordination model'),parts=[P('Explain the general role of the first two structures in endocrine coordination.',R('The hypothalamus integrates information and regulates pituitary activity.'),R('Pituitary signals can regulate other endocrine glands, coordinating hormone output.')),P('Explain why an alteration at the hypothalamic stage can affect several body functions.',A('Its control influences multiple endocrine pathways with different target tissues.')),P('State why receptor specificity remains necessary despite this broad coordination.',A('Only cells with suitable receptors respond to each particular hormone; circulation alone does not determine target response.'))])
b.add('C3.1','Pressure feedback to the heart','A constructed model records a sudden rise in arterial blood pressure, increased baroreceptor signalling and a subsequent reduction in heart rate.',refs=[14],kind='data-based',parts=[P('State a location of arterial baroreceptors and the variable they detect.',A('In the carotid sinus/carotid arteries or aortic arch.'),A('Stretch associated with arterial blood pressure.')),P('Explain the medulla’s role in the response.',R('It integrates receptor input and changes autonomic nerve signals to the heart.'),R('Reduced heart rate and/or contractility can reduce cardiac output, opposing the pressure rise.')),P('Explain why the response is negative feedback.',A('The response acts against the initial increase in pressure rather than amplifying it.'))])
b.add('C3.1','Carbon dioxide and ventilation','During a constructed exercise-recovery record, blood carbon dioxide rises and pH falls before ventilation increases. Carbon dioxide can cross into fluid around brainstem chemoreceptors and alter its pH.',refs=[15],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Explain the chemical link between increased carbon dioxide and lower pH.',R('Carbon dioxide reacts with water, increasing hydrogen-ion concentration through carbonic-acid/bicarbonate equilibria.')),P('Explain how chemoreceptor input can increase ventilation.',R('Brainstem centres respond to the chemical change and alter motor signalling to respiratory muscles.'),R('The diaphragm and intercostal muscles contract more frequently and/or strongly, increasing ventilation.')),P('Explain how the response helps restore the original conditions.',R('Increased ventilation removes more carbon dioxide, tending to reduce hydrogen-ion concentration and restore pH.'))])
b.add('C3.1','Coordinated movement through the gut','Which statement correctly contrasts control at the start of swallowing with control of most subsequent peristalsis?',refs=[16],difficulty='easy',options=['Swallowing can be initiated voluntarily; subsequent peristalsis is coordinated largely involuntarily by the enteric nervous system.','Every peristaltic contraction requires a conscious decision.','Peristalsis is coordinated only by voluntary motor signals from the cerebral hemispheres.','The enteric nervous system coordinates swallowing initiation but has no role farther along the intestine.'],reason='Voluntary initiation of swallowing involves the CNS, while the ENS coordinates involuntary waves of contraction along the digestive tract.')
shoot=SVG(box(240,285,200,55,'Growing medium')+'<path d="M340 285 Q340 205 395 150 Q420 128 450 115" fill="none" stroke="#41805c" stroke-width="12"/>'+line(340,285,340,90,'#8194a1',1,'5 5')+arrow(660,105,500,105,orange,3)+arrow(660,150,500,150,orange,3)+txt(650,70,'Light',18)+txt(260,90,'Initial vertical',14)+txt(380,380,'Original shoot outline after lateral illumination; not to scale.',14),'Original seedling shoot curving towards lateral light',760,405)
b.add('C3.1','Recording a directional growth response','The original drawing shows a shoot after illumination from the right. In a constructed repeat experiment, curvature angles for five independently grown shoots are 18°, 24°, 21°, 27° and 20°.',refs=[17,18],diagram=shoot,kind='data-based',parts=[P('Name the response and distinguish the qualitative and quantitative observations.',A('Positive phototropism: growth towards the light.'),A('“Curved towards the light” is qualitative; the angle measurements are quantitative.')),P('Calculate the mean curvature angle.',M('(18+24+21+27+20)/5.'),A('22°.')),P('Give one specific improvement to the precision of angle measurement.',A('Photograph all shoots from the same perpendicular viewpoint with a fixed reference axis and measure the shoot tangent using a calibrated angle tool. Accept an equally specific method.'))])
b.calc('mean shoot curvature','(18+24+21+27+20)/5',22,'degrees')
b.add('C3.1','Polar transport of a plant signal','A simplified tissue contains a row of plant cells. Auxin efflux carriers are concentrated at the same end of each cell. Auxin can enter the cells, but its directed exit depends on these carriers.',refs=[19,20],parts=[P('Explain how carrier positioning produces directional transport through the tissue.',R('Each cell preferentially exports auxin through the membrane region containing efflux carriers.'),R('Aligned carrier positions pass auxin from cell to cell in a consistent direction, building an uneven distribution.')),P('Predict the effect of distributing the efflux carriers evenly over all sides.',A('Directional transport and the resulting concentration gradient would be reduced or less organized.')),P('State why auxin is described as a phytohormone.',A('It is a plant signalling chemical that regulates growth, development or responses to stimuli.'))])
b.add('C3.1','Differential elongation in a shoot','In a shoot receiving lateral light, auxin becomes more concentrated on the shaded side. In this shoot, the relevant auxin concentration promotes cell elongation.',refs=[18,21],parts=[P('Explain the cellular mechanism connecting auxin with elongation.',R('Auxin promotes hydrogen-ion secretion into the apoplast/cell-wall space.'),R('Acidification promotes wall loosening, allowing turgor-driven cell expansion and elongation.')),P('Explain why the shoot bends towards the light.',R('Cells on the shaded side elongate more than cells on the illuminated side.'),R('The longer shaded side forces the shoot to curve towards the illuminated side.')),P('Explain why “auxin makes the illuminated side grow faster” would predict the wrong bending direction.',A('Greater length on the illuminated side would bend the shoot away from that side, contrary to the observed response.'))])
b.add('C3.1','Coordinating roots and shoots','Root tips and shoot tips exchange plant signals. Cytokinin is produced in root tips, while auxin is produced in shoot tips.',refs=[22],parts=[P('State the general transport direction of each signal in this root–shoot interaction.',A('Cytokinin is transported from roots towards shoots.'),A('Auxin is transported from shoots towards roots.')),P('Explain why two-way signalling can help integrate growth.',R('Growth of each region can respond to information from the other, helping coordinate root and shoot development rather than allowing fully independent growth.')),P('Explain why a hormone concentration alone may not uniquely predict the growth of a cultured tissue.',A('Responses depend on the balance or interaction of signals, tissue type and conditions, not simply one hormone in isolation.'))])
b.add('C3.1','A ripening signal spreads','In a constructed model, a ripening fruit releases ethylene. Ethylene stimulates ripening changes and increased ethylene production in nearby responsive fruits. A ventilated chamber shows slower spread of ripening than a sealed chamber.',refs=[23],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Explain the positive-feedback loop.',R('Ethylene stimulates ripening.'),R('Ripening stimulates further ethylene production, reinforcing the signal.')),P('Explain the chamber difference using the model.',R('Ventilation removes or dilutes ethylene, reducing the signal concentration reaching neighbouring fruits.')),P('State one control needed before attributing the observed difference only to ethylene removal.',A('Keep temperature, humidity and fruit maturity comparable; award one appropriate controlled factor.'))])

# C3.2: 097–112. All content in this topic is shared SL/HL.
b.add('C3.2','Recognizing an infectious cause','A disease can be transmitted by a biological agent that replicates in a host. Which set contains only groups that include human pathogens?',refs=[1],difficulty='easy',options=['Viruses, bacteria, fungi and protists.','Viruses, bacteria, fungi and pollen grains.','Bacteria, fungi, protists and malignant human cells.','Viruses, pollen grains, protists and inherited gene variants.'],reason='Human infectious diseases can be caused by members of several groups, including viruses, bacteria, fungi and protists; “pathogen” refers to disease-causing agents rather than to one taxonomic group.')
b.add('C3.2','The first barrier is more than a wall','Skin normally prevents many pathogens from entering the body. Mucous membranes protect other exposed surfaces.',refs=[2],parts=[P('Describe one physical and one chemical contribution of intact skin to defence.',A('A continuous layer of closely packed cells forms a physical barrier to entry.'),A('Skin secretions or their acidic conditions inhibit some microorganisms; accept a specific antimicrobial substance.')),P('Explain how mucus can help protect a respiratory surface.',R('It traps particles and microorganisms so they are less likely to reach underlying cells; ciliary clearance may be included.')),P('Explain why a deep cut increases infection risk even if adaptive immunity is unchanged.',R('It breaches the primary physical barrier, allowing pathogens to enter tissues more directly.'))])
b.add('C3.2','Closing a break in the skin','A simplified original clotting model starts with platelet activation at damaged tissue and ends with a stable mesh trapping blood cells.',refs=[3],parts=[P('Outline the sequence linking platelet activation to a fibrin mesh.',A('Activated platelets release factors that initiate or amplify a clotting cascade.'),A('The cascade produces active thrombin.'),A('Thrombin converts soluble fibrinogen into insoluble fibrin, forming a mesh.')),P('Explain how this helps both blood retention and defence.',R('The mesh traps blood cells and seals the cut, reducing blood loss and restricting pathogen entry.'))])
b.add('C3.2','Engulfing a pathogen','An original simplified sequence shows a phagocyte surrounding a bacterium, enclosing it in a vesicle and bringing that vesicle together with enzyme-containing organelles.',refs=[4,5],diagram=phagocytosis_svg(),parts=[P('Name the uptake process and explain the role of lysosomes.',A('Endocytosis/phagocytosis.'),R('Lysosomes supply hydrolytic enzymes that digest the engulfed material.')),P('Explain how phagocytes can reach an infection in tissue outside blood vessels.',A('They move out of the blood into tissues by amoeboid movement/diapedesis and move towards the infection.')),P('State why this is classified as innate rather than antigen-specific adaptive defence.',R('Phagocytes recognize broad pathogen-associated features and do not require prior production of a unique antibody response to that pathogen.'))])
b.add('C3.2','A specific antigen is recognized','A person contains many different B-lymphocyte clones. A new pathogen carries an antigen that binds strongly to receptors on only a small subset of them.',refs=[6,7,8],parts=[P('Explain why only a subset initially recognizes this antigen.',R('Different B-cell clones have different antigen-binding specificities, so only receptors with a complementary binding site bind this antigen effectively.')),P('State the additional cellular interaction required for the helper-T-cell-dependent response described here.',A('An activated helper T cell recognizing the same antigen type provides contact/signals that activate the matching B cell.')),P('Explain why a red-blood-cell surface antigen can also provoke an immune response after an incompatible transfusion.',R('An antigen can be recognized as foreign even when carried by a human cell rather than by a pathogen.'))])
b.add('C3.2','Clonal expansion after activation','One activated B-lymphocyte divides repeatedly to form a clone. Some descendants become antibody-secreting plasma cells and others persist as memory cells.',refs=[9,10],parts=[P('Explain how clonal expansion helps raise antibody concentration.',R('Mitosis increases the number of cells with the same antigen specificity.'),R('Many plasma cells can secrete large amounts of the corresponding antibody.')),P('Explain the advantage of retaining memory cells after the infection is cleared.',R('They survive and can respond rapidly when the same antigen is encountered again.'),R('Their expansion can produce a faster and larger specific response, reducing the time available for the pathogen to multiply.'))])
