b.add('C3.2','Comparing two antibody responses','The constructed graph compares antibody concentration after first exposure and after a later exposure to the same antigen. For each curve, day 0 is the day of that exposure; the later exposure occurred after the first response had subsided.',refs=[4,9,10],diagram=graph(immxs,[('First exposure',primary,blue),('Later exposure',secondary,orange)],'Time since the specified exposure / days','Antibody / arbitrary units','Constructed primary and secondary antibody response curves',xlim=(0,14),ylim=(0,140),xticks=[0,2,4,6,8,10,12,14],yticks=[0,35,70,105,140]),kind='data-based',parts=[P('Compare the responses using two features shown in the graph.',A('The later response begins sooner/reaches a substantial concentration sooner.'),A('It reaches a higher peak, about 120 versus 32 arbitrary units.')),P('Explain these differences in terms of adaptive immunity.',R('Memory lymphocytes from the first exposure are retained and rapidly activated on re-exposure.'),R('Their expansion and differentiation quickly generate many antibody-secreting cells.')),P('Explain why the graph does not show that the later response will be equally strong against an unrelated antigen.',A('Immunological memory is antigen-specific; an unrelated antigen may require activation of different clones.'))])
b.add('C3.2','Routes of HIV transmission','Which exposure provides a recognized route for HIV transmission?',refs=[11],difficulty='easy',options=['Shared injection equipment contaminated with infected blood.','Sitting beside a person in a classroom.','Shaking hands across intact skin.','Sharing ordinary air in a room.'],reason='HIV can be transmitted when infected blood enters another person through contaminated injection equipment. Casual contact and shared air do not provide this transmission route.')
b.add('C3.2','Loss of helper T cells','Untreated HIV infection can progressively reduce the number of functional CD4 helper T lymphocytes. A person may then become more susceptible to opportunistic infections.',refs=[12,8],parts=[P('Explain why loss of this cell type affects antibody-mediated defence.',R('Helper T cells provide signals needed to activate appropriate B lymphocytes.'),R('Fewer functional helper T cells can reduce effective B-cell activation and antibody production.')),P('Explain why HIV infection is not identical to an immediate diagnosis of AIDS.',A('AIDS is an advanced syndrome of immune deficiency; infection can precede severe immune impairment by a substantial period.')),P('Explain the meaning of “opportunistic” in this context.',A('The pathogens cause serious disease when weakened defences give them an opportunity, even if they are usually controlled in people with effective immunity.'))])
b.add('C3.2','Why a cell-wall antibiotic misses a virus','An antibiotic blocks bacterial peptidoglycan cell-wall synthesis. A student expects it to stop replication of an influenza virus in a human cell.',refs=[13],parts=[P('Explain why the antibiotic is selectively useful against susceptible bacteria.',R('Bacteria depend on peptidoglycan wall synthesis, a process absent from human cells.')),P('Explain why this mechanism does not directly inhibit influenza replication.',R('Viruses do not build a peptidoglycan cell wall.'),R('They replicate using host-cell machinery and viral processes that are not the antibiotic’s target.')),P('State why not every bacterium is necessarily sensitive to the antibiotic.',A('Some may lack effective access to the drug, possess resistance mechanisms or have a target not inhibited by it; award one valid reason.'))])
b.add('C3.2','Selection in an antibiotic-treated population','A constructed bacterial population initially contains a small proportion of cells with a resistance allele. After exposure to an antibiotic, the resistant proportion is much higher.',refs=[14],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Explain the change without claiming that the antibiotic deliberately produced useful mutations.',R('Susceptible cells are more likely to die or fail to reproduce under treatment.'),R('Resistant cells survive and reproduce more successfully, passing resistance to descendants and increasing its frequency.')),P('Explain how resistance to more than one antibiotic can accumulate in a lineage.',A('Variants carrying multiple resistance determinants can be selected; acquisition of resistance genes by horizontal transfer is also acceptable.')),P('Explain one reason for restricting unnecessary antibiotic use.',R('It reduces avoidable selection favouring resistant bacteria, helping slow their spread.'))])
b.add('C3.2','Recognizing zoonotic transmission','Three real disease examples involve different animal-to-human transmission routes: rabies can follow a bite from an infected mammal; Japanese encephalitis virus can be transmitted by mosquitoes from animal reservoirs; infection with Mycobacterium bovis can follow consumption of contaminated unpasteurized dairy products.',refs=[15],parts=[P('State the feature that makes all three examples zoonotic.',A('The infectious agent can transfer from another animal species to humans.')),P('Contrast the transmission routes in the first two examples.',A('Rabies: infected saliva is introduced through a bite/direct contact route.'),A('Japanese encephalitis: a mosquito acts as a biological vector between hosts.')),P('Explain why controlling only one route would not prevent all zoonoses.',R('Zoonotic pathogens use different reservoirs and routes, so a measure effective against one route may not interrupt the others.'))])
b.add('C3.2','Two vaccine formats','Vaccine X supplies a purified antigen. Vaccine Y supplies RNA encoding an antigen. Neither preparation contains a pathogen capable of causing the target disease.',refs=[16],parts=[P('Explain how both formats can initiate a specific adaptive response.',A('X directly introduces an antigen that can be recognized by the immune system.'),A('Cells use Y’s RNA to make the antigen, which can then be recognized.')),P('Explain the intended lasting outcome.',R('The response generates antigen-specific memory cells that can respond more effectively on later exposure.')),P('Explain why neither format needs to reproduce all the disease symptoms to work.',A('Immune recognition and memory formation can be stimulated by the antigen without the full disease-causing infection.'))])
b.add('C3.2','Protection through a community','In a constructed transmission model, immunization prevents infection in many recipients and therefore reduces the number of people who can pass the pathogen onward.',refs=[17],parts=[P('Explain how an unvaccinated person can benefit indirectly.',R('Fewer susceptible/infectious contacts reduce the probability that the pathogen reaches the person.'),R('Chains of transmission are interrupted, reducing community spread.')),P('State two reasons why this indirect protection is not a guarantee for every person.',A('Immunization may be incomplete or protection may wane; accept reduced effectiveness against a changed pathogen.'),A('Susceptible people may cluster or infection may be introduced into an unprotected group. Award a distinct reason.')),P('Explain why a vaccine that prevents severe disease but barely reduces infection would provide less of this particular indirect effect.',R('Vaccinated people could still become infected and transmit, so transmission chains would be interrupted less effectively.'))])
b.add('C3.2','A simulated epidemic record','The table contains invented practice data, not actual COVID-19 statistics. Two districts recorded confirmed infections over the same month using comparable testing procedures.'+T(['District','Population','Confirmed cases'],[['P',40000,240],['Q',100000,400]]),refs=[18],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Calculate confirmed cases per 100000 people in each district.',A('P: 240/40000 ×100000 = 600.'),A('Q: 400/100000 ×100000 = 400.')),P('Explain why comparing only the case counts gives a different impression.',R('Q has more cases in total but a larger population; P has the higher population-standardized rate.')),P('Give one reason why confirmed-case rates may differ from true infection rates even with comparable procedures.',A('Not all infected individuals are tested or detected; asymptomatic infection, access or test sensitivity can affect ascertainment.'))])
