b.add('C3.2','Percentage change and percentage difference','A simulated public-health record, created only for practice, reports 120 cases in week 1 and 90 in week 2 for the same population. A separate comparison has 80 cases in group A and 100 in equally sized group B. Use percentage difference = absolute difference divided by the mean of the two values, multiplied by 100.',refs=[18],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Calculate the percentage change from week 1 to week 2.',M('(90−120)/120 ×100.'),A('−25%, or a 25% decrease.')),P('Calculate the specified percentage difference between groups A and B.',M('Absolute difference = 20; mean = 90; use 20/90 ×100.'),A('22.2% to three significant figures.')),P('Explain why the two calculations use different denominators.',R('Percentage change uses the starting value as a reference, whereas the supplied symmetric percentage-difference definition uses the mean of the two values.'))])
