# C4.1: 113–128.
b.add('C4.1','Population or community','A pond contains one interbreeding group of frogs, several species of plants, aquatic insects, fungi and bacteria. Which description is correct?',refs=[1,10],difficulty='easy',options=['The frogs form a population; all the interacting populations form the community.','All frog species in the pond form one population because they occupy the same habitat.','The frog population includes every frog species worldwide regardless of isolation.','The community includes the interacting organisms and also the pond water and mineral sediment.'],reason='A population consists of interacting members of the same species in an area. A community includes the interacting populations of all species, including plants and microorganisms.')
b.add('C4.1','Scaling up quadrat counts','Eight randomly located 0.25 m² quadrats in a 600 m² meadow contain the following constructed counts of a small sessile plant: 2, 3, 0, 1, 4, 2, 3 and 1. The sample SD of these counts is 1.31 plants per quadrat.',refs=[2,3],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Estimate the total population in the meadow.',M('Mean count = 16/8 = 2 plants per 0.25 m².'),M('Density = 2/0.25 = 8 plants m⁻².'),A('Estimated population = 8×600 = 4800 plants.')),P('Interpret the supplied SD and state why this is an estimate rather than an exact census.',A('Counts vary among quadrats by an amount summarized by the SD; it describes spatial variation, not a guaranteed error bound on the total.'),R('Only part of the meadow was sampled, so random sampling error can make the sample mean differ from the true mean.')),P('Describe how to choose quadrat positions without favouring visibly dense patches.',A('Use random coordinates across a mapped grid of the entire meadow before examining local plant density.'))])
b.calc('quadrat population estimate','(2+3+0+1+4+2+3+1)/8/.25*600',4800,'plants')
b.add('C4.1','A mark–recapture estimate','In a constructed closed-population study, 40 beetles are captured, marked harmlessly and released. After mixing, a second sample contains 50 beetles, of which 10 are marked. Use the Lincoln estimate N = first marked × second sample / marked recaptured.',refs=[4],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Estimate population size.',M('N = 40×50/10.'),A('200 beetles.')),P('State two assumptions needed for the estimate.',A('The population is effectively closed between samples: no substantial births, deaths, immigration or emigration.'),A('Marked animals mix fully and are as likely to be recaptured as unmarked animals; accept persistent recognizable marks that do not affect survival/capture as a distinct assumption.')),P('Explain why releasing the marked animals at one point immediately before recapture could bias the estimate.',R('They may not mix randomly, so the marked fraction in the second sample may not represent the whole population.'))])
b.calc('Lincoln population estimate','40*50/10',200,'beetles')
b.add('C4.1','When marks change recapture','A second constructed study marks 60 animals, then captures 80 in the later sample. Only 8 marked animals are recognized. Some animals have lost their marks between samples.',refs=[4],kind='data-based',difficulty='hard',parts=[P('Calculate the estimate using the observed counts.',A('60×80/8 = 600 animals.')),P('Predict the direction of bias caused by mark loss and explain it.',A('The estimate is biased upwards/too high.'),R('Mark loss lowers the recognized marked-recapture count in the denominator, making the calculated population larger.')),P('Propose an improvement that addresses this specific problem without assuming perfect marks.',A('Test mark retention over the relevant interval or use a validated, persistent, harmless marking method and account for measured mark loss.'))])
b.calc('mark-loss affected estimate','60*80/8',600,'animals')
b.add('C4.1','Density and a moving carrying capacity','A constructed island population grows until competition for food increases mortality and reduces reproduction. A storm later destroys half the food-producing vegetation.',refs=[5,6],parts=[P('Define carrying capacity in this context.',A('The population size that the environment can sustain over time under the prevailing resource and environmental conditions.')),P('Explain how food competition can provide negative feedback on population size.',R('At higher density, less food is available per individual.'),R('Reduced survival or reproduction slows growth or decreases population size, opposing the increase.')),P('Explain why the storm does not demonstrate that carrying capacity is always constant.',A('The storm changes resource availability, so the sustainable population size can decrease.')),P('Distinguish the storm from density-dependent pathogen transmission.',A('The storm is an environmental disturbance whose occurrence is not determined by population density; transmission can become more frequent as host density rises.'))])
b.add('C4.1','Testing an exponential model','Constructed counts in a newly established population are 25, 50, 100 and 200 on days 0, 2, 4 and 6 respectively.',refs=[7],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Explain why the data are consistent with exponential rather than constant additive growth.',R('Population size doubles over each equal two-day interval, so the proportional increase is constant.'),R('The absolute increments increase (25, 50, 100) rather than remaining constant.')),P('State the expected pattern if log population size is plotted against time.',A('An approximately straight line with positive slope.')),P('Explain one biological condition that could permit the early pattern and one reason it may not persist.',A('Initially abundant resources and little competition can permit rapid reproduction.'),A('Later resource limitation or another density-dependent factor can reduce per-capita growth.'))])
