b.add('C4.1','Food and predation in the Yukon','In the real Kluane snowshoe-hare experiment, researchers independently manipulated food supply and mammalian predator access. The [1995 research paper]('+hare_source+') reports approximate density multipliers during the population peak and decline, summarized below. These are reported results, not invented counts.'+T(['Treatment','Hare density relative to control'],[['Control',1],['Predator exclusion',2],['Added food',3],['Both treatments',11]]),refs=[16,17],kind='data-based',difficulty='hard',parts=[P('Identify which manipulation tests top-down control and which tests bottom-up control.',A('Predator exclusion tests top-down control.'),A('Food addition tests bottom-up control.')),P('Calculate the prediction if the two increases above the control were simply additive, and compare it with the combined result.',M('Control 1 plus the predator-removal increment (2−1) plus the food increment (3−1).'),A('Predicted multiplier = 4; the observed combined multiplier of about 11 is much larger.')),P('Explain what the combined result suggests about using a single-factor explanation.',R('Food supply and predation interact, so either factor alone gives an incomplete explanation of hare density under these conditions.')),P('Explain one way in which prey density can feed back on predator populations.',R('More prey can increase predator survival or reproduction, increasing later predation pressure; fewer prey can have the reverse effect.'))],notes='Publicly reported quantitative summary from Krebs et al. 1995, Science 269:1112–1115. Original questions and analysis; no source figures reused.')
b.calc('additive density multiplier','1+(2-1)+(3-1)',4,'relative to control')
b.add('C4.1','Contrasting community controls','In a constructed lake study, adding nutrients increases algae and then herbivore biomass. In another lake, reducing fish predators increases herbivores and decreases algae.',refs=[17],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Classify the dominant direction of control illustrated in each case.',A('Nutrient addition illustrates bottom-up control.'),A('Predator reduction illustrates top-down control.')),P('Explain the decrease in algae in the second lake.',R('Reduced predation allows more herbivores to survive.'),R('Greater herbivory removes more algae, producing an indirect effect across trophic levels.')),P('State why these results do not show that only one control direction can ever operate in a lake.',A('Both resources and consumers can affect populations simultaneously, and their relative importance can change with conditions.'))])
b.add('C4.1','Chemical suppression of neighbours','Black walnut produces juglone, which can inhibit plant growth in laboratory assays. Penicillium fungi can release penicillin, which inhibits susceptible bacteria. A constructed assay shows reduced seedling growth with purified juglone, while a separate assay shows reduced bacterial growth with penicillin.',refs=[18],kind='data-based',parts=[P('Name the ecological process illustrated by chemical inhibition of neighbouring plants and the class of chemical in the fungal example.',A('Allelopathy.'),A('An antibiotic.')),P('Explain the shared potential benefit to the organism producing the chemical.',R('Suppressing competitors can reduce competition for resources such as space or nutrients.')),P('Explain why the juglone assay alone does not establish that juglone causes all poor growth beneath walnut trees in the field.',R('Laboratory concentration and exposure may differ from field conditions, where shade, roots, water and nutrients also affect growth.')),P('State one piece of additional evidence needed to connect the laboratory effect to a field mechanism.',A('Measure bioavailable juglone at inhibitory concentrations in the relevant field soil, or manipulate juglone independently of shade/root competition and measure plant response.'))],notes='Laboratory-versus-field distinction is intentional. Chemical potential does not by itself prove field causality.')

