b.add('C4.2','Separating seasonal and long-term carbon signals','The graph is an original constructed teaching model of two features seen in the Keeling Curve: seasonal oscillation and a rising long-term baseline. These are not actual monitoring measurements. Month 0 is May in a Northern Hemisphere year; months 12 and 24 are the following Mays.',refs=[20],diagram=graph(kx,[('Model CO₂',ky,blue)],'Months since the first May','Atmospheric CO₂ / ppm','Constructed model of seasonal and long-term atmospheric carbon patterns',xlim=(0,24),ylim=(400,420),xticks=[0,6,12,18,24],yticks=[400,405,410,415,420],note='Constructed values, not observations from a monitoring station.'),kind='data-based',parts=[P('Calculate the average annual rise between the first and third May.',M('(416−410)/2 years.'),A('3 ppm yr⁻¹.')),P('Explain a biological reason for the recurring seasonal declines.',R('During the growing season, increased photosynthesis on the extensive Northern Hemisphere land area can remove more carbon dioxide than is released locally by respiration and decomposition.')),P('Explain why seasonal falls do not contradict a long-term rise.',R('Seasonal uptake and release can occur around a baseline that rises when total additions, including combustion, exceed removals over longer periods.')),P('Explain why comparing the first May with the following November would give a misleading estimate of the annual trend.',A('Those months represent different seasonal phases; the difference would mix seasonal fluctuation with the long-term change.'))])
b.calc('same-season annual CO2 rise','(416-410)/2',3,'ppm yr⁻¹')
b.add('C4.2','Matter cycles while energy flows','A sealed transparent microcosm contains plants, decomposers, mineral nutrients, water and air. It receives light and transfers heat to its surroundings, but no matter is deliberately added or removed.',refs=[1,2,21,22],parts=[P('Classify the microcosm as open, closed or isolated using the stated boundary conditions.',A('Closed to matter but open to energy; it is a closed system, not an isolated one.')),P('Explain the role of decomposers in sustaining mineral availability.',R('They break down organic matter and release mineral nutrients into forms that can be reused by producers.')),P('Explain why recycling carbon alone would not be sufficient.',R('Organisms also require other elements, such as nitrogen, phosphorus and sulfur, which must be available and recycled.')),P('Explain why the microcosm still needs an energy input despite this material recycling.',R('Energy is dissipated as heat during metabolism and cannot be recycled into an equivalent usable biological supply; light replenishes the input.')),P('State why a sealed setup is not guaranteed to remain biologically stable indefinitely.',A('Gas balance, nutrient availability, species interactions or physical conditions may become unsuitable even if matter is retained; award one developed reason.'))])

b.save()
